NAME
       tail - print the last lines of files, or follow a growing file

SYNOPSIS
       tail [OPTION]... [FILE]...

DESCRIPTION
       tail prints the last 10 lines of each FILE, or of standard input when
       no file is given. With several files it prints a header line
       "==> FILE <==" before each.

       With -f, tail keeps the file open after printing and shows new lines
       as they are written: the usual way to watch a log while you test
       something in another window. Press Ctrl+C to stop.

OPTIONS
       -n, --lines=N
              Print the last N lines instead of 10. -n +N starts at line N
              and prints everything after it (tail -n +2 skips a header line).
              The old form -N (tail -5) works too.

       -c, --bytes=N
              Print the last N bytes.

       -f, --follow
              Keep printing lines as they are added.

       -F     Like -f, and keep following when the file is replaced or
              recreated (as log rotation does).

       --pid=PID
              With -f, stop when process PID ends.

       -q, --quiet
              Never print the file name headers.

       -v, --verbose
              Always print the file name headers.

EXAMPLES
       Show the newest lines of the security log:

           tail /var/log/secure

       Watch SELinux denials appear while you test a web page:

           tail -f /var/log/audit/audit.log

       Print the last 3 accounts created:

           tail -n 3 /etc/passwd

       Print a CSV file without its first (header) line:

           tail -n +2 /root/report.csv

       Show lines 11 to 20 of a file:

           head -n 20 /etc/services | tail -n 10

NOTES
       journalctl -f does for the journal what tail -f does for a log file.

SEE ALSO
       head(1), cat(1), less(1), journalctl(1)
